{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261110T021638Z-269e1e06", "ts": "2026-11-10T02:30:50Z", "actor": {"worktree": "cordyceps", "plugin": "3.8.1-dev", "branch": "feat/canvas-read", "build": "52bb14851a37"}, "body": {"base_commit": "975912d7deda46dd6bb086bfab0e5d8f6ee519d9", "base_tree": "13c56db4bca0a35df1137af2ff1bb35fd0005ee9", "head_tree": "f641a7ac1ca8929489ff94c81ad1c9211f2ee60c", "head_commit": "20f4958ad1a59a97c1aa4d66fd4456d71889093b", "dispatch_commit": "20f4958ad1a59a97c1aa4d66fd4456d71889093b", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}], "files_reviewed": ["CHANGELOG.md", "CLAUDE.md", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/CanvasOutlineTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/CanvasSnapshotTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/GuidPrefixIndexTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/IdBatchTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectHealthTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TreeSummaryTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Cordyceps.csproj", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/GuidPrefixIndex.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/DataTreeReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeAlignment.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeSummary.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/BestPracticesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/DebugDataMismatch.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/OptimizeCanvas.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Resources/ResourceRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Outline.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Values.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/changes/canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "CLAUDE.md", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/CanvasOutlineTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/CanvasSnapshotTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/GuidPrefixIndexTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/IdBatchTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectHealthTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/TreeSummaryTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Cordyceps.csproj", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/GuidPrefixIndex.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/DataTreeReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeAlignment.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeSummary.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/BestPracticesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/DataTreesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/DebugDataMismatch.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/OptimizeCanvas.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Resources/ResourceRegistry.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Groups.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Outline.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Values.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/changes/canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "move reports success for a repeated object while dropping that entry's coordinates", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (move is the one many-id action whose entries carry their own payload; delete, preview and enable are idempotent, so 'act once' is honest there). Why it broke: C1.7's act-once rule was applied to moves unchanged, although two move entries for one object are two different requests. In ActionMove (src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs, the `!outcome.ActOn` branch) a later entry naming an object an earlier entry already moved returns `{success: true, id, note}` and its x/y are discarded, so `moves=[{id:A,x:0,y:0},{id:'<A by nickname>',x:100,y:100}]` leaves A at 0,0 and reports two successes; before this interval the same call left A at 100,100 (last entry won). The mirror case also misbehaves: when the earlier entry failed only on its x/y, a later, fully valid entry for the same object is refused with 'same object as entry i, which was not moved'. An agent that sends two positions for one object (once by id, once by name) now believes the second position applied. Nothing exercises this: the pairing of outcomes with coordinates and the repeat handling live in the host file with no unit test, and VRF-019 has no step with two moves for one object. This is the pattern the learning 'Validate-then-mutate, and never conflate \"unparseable\" with \"empty\"' warns about on both counts (a skip reported as success:true; pure decision logic left in a host-coupled file without tests). Fix: for move, either apply each resolved entry in order (so the last position wins, as before) or fail the later entry with an error when its coordinates differ from the entry it repeats; move the per-entry plan (id outcome + coordinate outcome -> act / report) into a host-free Core file with tests; and if first-wins is kept, list it under a 'Breaking' bullet in CHANGELOG.md and correct the CommonErrorsGuide row that says a `note: \"same object as entry 0\"` on `moves` is 'Not an error: the object was acted on once'.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs", "CHANGELOG.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "date-shaped names fail in moves, connections and outline group arrays (fixed only in tree)", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: Json.NET turns ISO date-time-shaped strings into Date tokens unless DateParseHandling.None is set, and only the tree parser sets it. TreeRequest.cs reads `ids` with DateParseHandling.None and pins it with Parse_Ids_DateShapedTextStaysAnId ('a nickname can look like one'), but three other places that now accept names parse with the default: (1) OutlineRequest.TryParseGroups uses JArray.Parse, so `group='[\"2026-01-01T00:00:00\"]'` is refused with 'group[0] must be a string ..., got date' although the overview printed that group name; (2) ActionMove reads `moves` through TryDeserializeList<dynamic>, and (3) ActionConnect reads `connections` through JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<List<dynamic>>, so `id`/`sourceId`/`targetId` values of that shape reach the resolver as the current-culture rendering of a DateTime ('No object named '1/1/2026 12:00:00 AM'.'), which also breaks the plan's invariant-culture constraint. The Product goal 'every name the outline prints works as an id in every tool that takes that kind of thing' has a hole at each site, and the round-trip golden test cannot see it because it calls the resolver directly, after parsing. The learning 'Tracing consumers of a shared helper misses paths that bypass it' applies: trace by the behaviour. Bound the class by searching src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified and src/Cordyceps/Core for JArray.Parse, JToken.Parse, JObject.Parse and DeserializeObject<List<dynamic>> on any argument that can carry an object or group name; expect members outside this diff. Close it by construction: one host-free JSON reader for name-carrying arguments (DateParseHandling.None, as TreeRequest already does) that all of these sites pass through, with a test per site's shape; OutlineRequest is already linked into the test project, so its case can be pinned directly.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineRequest.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhWireTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeRequest.cs"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Two caller-visible changes are not under a Breaking bullet in the changelog", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (two sites, both found by diffing every Tools/Unified file and ToolHelpers.cs against the base and checking each changed outcome against [Unreleased]). Why: the Breaking bullets were transcribed from the plan's Chunk 05 list, and these two follow from Chunk 02 and C1.7 without being on that list. (1) `gh_wire(action='connect')`, single and bulk, with a full GUID that names nothing: the entry's error was `Component not found` and is now `Source: Component not found: {guid}` / `Target: ...`. The 'changed error texts' bullet is scoped to 'ids that are not full GUIDs' and calls out the same before-text for `move` only. (2) A repeated id in `delete` / `preview` / `enable`: the later entry used to fail (`Component not found: {guid}` on delete, overall success false) and now succeeds with a note. This is stated, but only inside the 'Added' names bullet, not as a behaviour change. Neither touches the contract surface boundary-patterns.md names (tool names, actions, parameters, response shape), so this is a completeness note, not a norm departure. Every other changed outcome I enumerated is announced: group_move and list group= exact matching; member arrays all-or-nothing, notMembers, self-membership; gh_wire list unknown id; retired non-GUID texts; move's three texts; Group not found / Object is not a group; resolved-GUID echoes at every site (no site still echoes caller text on success); gh_inspect limit default; infrastructure texts on move, connect and group_move; negative log limit; JSON null on ids, connections and items. No action or parameter was removed or renamed. Add (1) to the error-text bullet and (2) to a Breaking bullet.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Resolution.MatchedBy has no production reader", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. ObjectResolver sets MatchedBy (Guid, GuidPrefix, Nickname, GroupName) on every resolution, and the only reader is one test line; no tool or view branches on it. The plan's C1 specifies it deliberately ('the enum is open so batch can add Ref'), and batch is out of this wave, so this is recorded rather than a defect: nothing anyone does today goes wrong because of it. No edit is wanted unless the batch wave is dropped, in which case delete the field and enum.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "No live-host evidence yet for the id wiring, tree or outline adapters", "recommendation": "Scope: none (a mandated check carrying no defect in the diff). The host-free rules are well tested, but CanvasReader, the routing of every id site through ToolHelpers, the tree action and the outline action have only been compiled: VRF-019, VRF-020 and VRF-021 are all 'pending', and the plan's unverified host facts (whether nested groups appear in GH_Group.ObjectIDs, default-nickname detection, which solver switch each route sets, input VolatileData after modifiers) decide what outline and tree print. The plan chose this route explicitly and the queue entries exist, so it is not a dropped requirement. Because this interval is a breaking change to every id-taking action, treat discharging the three VRF entries as a condition of the release PR to main rather than of this merge to develop, and say so in the PR body.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Docs say status lists every problem, but status reads components only while outline also lists parameter objects", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: outline and status share the classifier (ObjectHealth) but not the population, and the documents were written from the classifier alone. `ActionStatus` skips everything that is not an `IGH_Component` (src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs, `if (!(obj is IGH_Component comp)) continue;`), while `OutlineView.View.Problems()` and `Summary()` walk every node (src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs:488-506, 635-644), so a slider, panel or free parameter carrying an error or warning is in outline's `problems` and its `E errors \u00b7 W warnings` counts and is absent from status. Eight places tell the agent the opposite: McpServer.cs:699 ('status lists every one'), BestPracticesGuide.md:50 ('every one'), CanvasOutlineGuide.md:8, 112, 114 and 214, GettingStartedGuide.md:31-32 ('the full problem list'), Prompts/OptimizeCanvas.md:9, the outline ActionInfo tip (GhCanvasTool.Outline.cs:33), and the `next` text outline itself emits (OutlineView.cs:96). Consequence: an agent that sees `more.problems` follows the instruction, runs status, and never sees the parameter-object problems past the cap; it also cannot reconcile outline's warning count with status's `summary.warning`. Close it one of two ways and say which: (a) keep status as it is (the plan requires its output unchanged) and make every one of those sentences relational, e.g. 'status lists every component; errors and warnings on parameter objects appear only in outline', with the `next` text and the guide's rule paragraph saying the same; or (b) give both one population owner (status walking the same set outline does), which is an owner decision because it changes status output. To find every member: grep 'full list', 'every one', 'full problem list', 'same rule' and 'lists everything' across src/Cordyceps and CHANGELOG.md.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/BestPracticesGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/OptimizeCanvas.md", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Outline.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The object-name rule has two owners: the full-GUID branch is written three times in ToolHelpers beside ObjectResolver, and many-id handling twice", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the full-GUID path was kept outside the snapshot for cost, and it was kept as parallel code rather than as an index the resolver reads. Sites: rule 2 (text that parses as a GUID) lives in `ObjectResolver.Decide` (src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs:247-263, texts at 311-316) and again in src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs at 341-350 (`TryGetUnprotectedComponentWithDoc`), 366-375 (the `ObjectLookup` overload, a line-for-line copy of the first) and 409-439 (`TryGetUnprotectedGroup`, with its own copies of `Group not found: \u2026` and `Object is not a group: \u2026`). Only outline sends a full GUID through the resolver; every other tool takes the ToolHelpers copy, which reads `doc.FindObject` where the resolver reads the snapshot built from `GetActiveObjects`. Rule 7 (many ids, repeats marked) is likewise written twice: `ObjectResolver.ResolveMany` + `ResolvedEntry` (ObjectResolver.cs:105-128, 168-185; used only by outline `group=`) and `IdBatch.Collect` + `IdOutcome` (src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs:8-76; used by delete, move, preview, enable and the member arrays), down to the same `same object as entry i` string at ObjectResolver.cs:127 and IdBatch.cs:35. Consequences: CLAUDE.md now tells a maintainer to 'change name resolution in the resolver, not at a call site', and a change to the GUID rule or its text made there takes effect for outline only; and the plan's stated reason for `IObjectIndex` (a batch overlay decorates the index and the rules stay put) does not hold for full GUIDs, which never reach the index from any ToolHelpers choke point. Close it by construction, keeping today's behaviour and cost: make `ObjectLookup` an `IObjectIndex` whose `Find`/`IsInfrastructure` answer from the document and whose name and prefix lookups build the Names snapshot on first use, so the three ToolHelpers methods call `ObjectResolver.Resolve` for every text and the fast path becomes a property of the index; `IdBatch.Collect` then reduces to `ResolveMany`. If that is deferred, at least fold the two identical Objects-scope GUID branches into one and record the deferral where the batch wave will find it. Bound: grep `TryParseGuid(` in src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs; I found no Grasshopper-object site under src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified or src/Cordyceps/Core that parses a GUID or matches a nickname outside these.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs", "CLAUDE.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The missing-input rule is still written a second time in gh_inspect disconnected, and tree reads the message level on its own", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the plan moved the rule out of `ActionStatus` only, and the consumer trace followed status rather than the predicate. `ActionDisconnected` (src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs:352) still evaluates `!input.Optional && input.SourceCount == 0 && input.VolatileDataCount == 0` itself, the same predicate `ObjectHealth.Classify` owns (src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs:93). A second, smaller bypass: `DescribeTrees` decides an owner's problem level from `RuntimeMessageLevel >= Warning` directly (src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs:149-154) instead of through `CanvasReader.Health`. Consequence: the next change to what counts as a missing input (the `HealthInput.SourceCount` comment already marks one subtle choice, that infrastructure sources count) is made in ObjectHealth, status and outline follow it, and `gh_inspect(action='disconnected')` keeps answering by the old rule for the same canvas. Have `ActionDisconnected` take its inputs from `CanvasReader.Health(comp).MissingInputs` (or a shared `ObjectHealth` predicate over `HealthInput` when it needs the index and type per input), and have tree read the level and messages from the same `HealthResult`. Bound: grep `SourceCount == 0` and `RuntimeMessageLevel` under src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified; these two are the only members I found (GhScriptTool.cs:495 and ToolHelpers.cs:800/839 only report the level, they do not classify). This applies the project learning 'Tracing consumers of a shared helper misses paths that bypass it': trace by the behaviour, not by the helper's callers.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "CLAUDE.md project content is about 194 lines, over the ~150 guideline", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: each change adds a paragraph and none removes one; this bundle added the Core/Canvas paragraph (one line of roughly 900 characters) on top of a file already over the line. The file is 219 lines; outside the PRAWDUCT anchor section (lines 165-188) that is about 194 lines of project content. Consequence: every session pays for it in context, and the densest paragraphs are the ones least likely to be read closely. Candidates to move, each already having a home: 'Branch model & Publishing' (lines 129-163) duplicates docs/release-process.md and can shrink to the branch rule plus a pointer; the worked C# example under 'Adding or Modifying Tools' (lines 85-98) can move to a docs page; the backlog section's recovery command and migration detail (lines 207-219) belong in .prawduct/artifacts/migration-scrub-decisions.md, which the section already links.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The per-entry loop for a many-id action is written four times and its results have already diverged", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `IdBatch` owns resolving and marking repeats but not reporting them, so each action wrote its own loop. The same three-way branch (unresolved, repeat of an earlier entry, act) appears in `ActionDelete` and `ActionMove` (src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs, around 653-678 and 776-799) and, through `AddRepeatResult`, in `ActionPreview` and `ActionEnable` (src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Values.cs:215-245, 275-307, 330-341). A repeat whose first entry failed reads `\u2026, which was not deleted` with a `note` field on delete, `\u2026, which was not moved` with no `note` on move, and `\u2026, which failed` with a `note` on preview/enable. Nothing breaks today; an agent sees three shapes for one condition. A single host-free helper beside `IdBatch` that turns an `IdOutcome` plus 'did the first entry succeed' into the result entry would give the four loops one shape. Filed as a note because the difference is small and each loop is correct on its own.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Values.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/IdBatch.cs"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Solver-off wording has one owner, in a class named for trees; three smaller text rules are duplicated around it", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: Chunk 03 wrote the wording for tree before outline existed, and outline reached into it rather than the wording moving to shared ground. The solver question itself comes out well: `TreeStatusText.SolverOff` (src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs:32-46) is the only place the three 'off: \u2026' sentences are written, both tree and outline call it, and I found no other solver-off message under Tools/Unified or Core. What is left: (1) outline composes `on; the last solution was aborted` itself (src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs:519) because `SolverOff` returns null when the solver is on, so the '; the last solution was aborted' fragment is written twice, and `Core/Canvas` now depends on `Core/DataTrees` for a sentence about `SolverFlags`, which lives in Canvas; the class summary still says these are the words tree uses. (2) 'first message, clipped, (+K more)' is written twice with different clips and prefixes: `OutlineView.View.Messages` (100 characters, `error: \u2026`) and `TreeStatusText.FormatMessage` (160 characters, `Error: \u2026`). (3) Clipping is written three times: `OutlineView.Clip` and `CanvasText.Cap` refuse to split a surrogate pair, `FormatMessage`'s `Substring(0, MessageClip - 1)` does not. (4) Solution phase is mapped twice: `CanvasReader.ToPhase` and `GhInspectTool.Tree.OwnerStateOf`. A small host-free `SolverText`/`CanvasText` home for the solver sentence (covering the on-and-aborted case), one clip and one message formatter would remove all four. No edit is required for this bundle to be coherent.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhInspectTool.Tree.cs"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "OutlineView's size is mostly one responsibility; the graph-ordering algorithm is the separable part", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Answering the question asked: of the file's 909 lines, about 510 are the private `View` (the line grammar), about 110 the three entry points and 60 the text helpers; these are one responsibility, rendering three views of one grammar over one snapshot, and they share the name, health and wire caches, so splitting them would cost more than it buys. The exception is `FlowOrder` with `StronglyConnected` and `OrderCounter` (src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/OutlineView.cs:186-335, about 150 lines): a general upstream-first ordering over ids and edges that knows nothing about outline text, is public, and carries a counter parameter that exists only so a test can bound its cost. Moving it to its own host-free file would leave OutlineView as the grammar alone and let the algorithm's tests stand apart from the golden strings. Related, same file family: `CanvasNode.OutputFor`, `GuidPrefixIndex.Count`, `CanvasText.Number(double)`, `CanvasWire.TargetInput` and `Resolution.MatchedBy` have no production reader (tests only), and `OutlineView.View` re-derives `OutputFor`'s -1-to-0 mapping 